# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx man [-w] [section] title [node]
vx man --check
vx man --missing
vx man --index file
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx man` reads the manual in the tree, `man/<section>/<page>`, before an image boots.
It runs from the repository's root.

With a <title>, it prints the page that documents that name, in the first section that has one, or in <section>; with a <node> too, that node of the page alone.
The page is refilled to `$COLUMNS` columns, or 80, and indented as Plan 9's pages are, NAME made from the header and SOURCE from its `src`.
With `-w` it prints the page's path instead.

: `--check`
  Checks the manual against itself and against the code, as `vx ci` does after its builds, and fails if anything is wrong.
: `--missing`
  Prints rows for what needs a page and has neither a page nor a row in `man/missing`.
: `--index`
  Writes the index to <file>.

## What the check checks

- Every page parses (guide(6)) and lives at the path its header names.
- The house rules: no trailing spaces, no tabs outside fences, fences within 80 columns, headings in capitals and Plan 9's in Plan 9's order.
- No two pages give the same name in one section.
- Every link resolves: to a page through the index, to a node of it, or to a page `man/missing` promises.
- Everything that needs a page has one: each program in a built boot image's `bin/` (in section 1, 3, 4 or 8), each `vx` command and host tool (1 or 8), each syscall, public declaration of Sys and function a libc header declares (2), each driver (3), service (4 or 8), IDL protocol (5) and format (6), and each section's intro.
  The boot's own test programs need none.
- A format with a directive table, such as manifest(6), defines every directive in it.
- `man/missing` lists only what needs a page and has none.

## The backlog

`man/missing` holds what needs a page and has none: a row each, its kind and its name, a tab between.
The check fails on anything undocumented that isn't there, on a row whose page now exists, and on a row for something that no longer needs one, so the list only shrinks.
A change that adds something adds its page, not a row.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% vx man 6 guide fences
% vx man -w driver
man/6/driver
```

# FILES

: `man/<section>/<page>`
  The pages.
: `man/missing`
  The backlog.
: `build/<arch>/bootfs.img`
  The boot images whose programs need pages; with none built, programs aren't checked.

# SEE ALSO

guide(6), intro(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

Each problem is a line, `where: what`, such as `man/6/driver:12: a trailing space`; `--check` then prints how many it found and exits 1 if any.

# BUGS

`man`, `lookman` and `sig` don't run on VectraOS itself, and bootfs doesn't carry the pages: the manual is read on the dev machine only.
